[2026-03-04T20:30:06.761Z] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01W7gRGcg3e2adGKHGXaa6tq","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Skill\n     2→\n     3→You are writing playwright tests which check that all the different entries in docs/tests.md are satisfied by the app.\n     4→\n     5→## Unpack Subtasks\n     6→\n     7→Unpack the initial write tests task into subtasks using `add-task`. Add one task per page,\n     8→containing all test entries for that page:\n     9→\n    10→```\n    11→npx tsx /repo/scripts/add-task.ts --skill \"skills/tasks/build/writeTests.md\" --app \"<AppName>\" \\\n    12→  --subtask \"WriteTest<TestEntry1>: Write test for <TestEntry1>\" \\\n    13→  --subtask \"WriteTest<TestEntry2>: Write test for <TestEntry2>\" \\\n    14→  --subtask \"WriteTest<TestEntry3>: Write test for <TestEntry3>\"\n    15→```\n    16→\n    17→## Guidelines\n    18→\n    19→- For a given test entry, write a playwright test which matches the entry's requirements and verifies that the app will behave as the user expects.\n    20→- Do not run the playwright tests, but make sure that the test should pass and update the app code if necessary.\n    21→- The title of the playwright test must match the title of the test entry.\n    22→- If you discover parts of the app that haven't been fully implemented, you must finish implementation of the app.\n    23→- Playwright tests and app components/pages must use data-testid to identify elements on the page.\n    24→\n    25→## Directives\n    26→\n    27→- Tests must verify navigation targets precisely — assert the URL or page content after a click, not just that a click handler exists. A link going to the wrong page is a common bug that only surfaces if the test checks where it actually lands.\n    28→\n    29→- When a test spec entry implies a component is available across multiple pages (e.g., a sequential navigation flow that clicks sidebar links after navigating away from the starting page), tests must interact with that component on each page it should appear on. Never use `page.goto()` to navigate back to a known page as a workaround for a missing component — this masks the bug instead of catching it. If the spec says \"click X in the sidebar\" after navigating to a different page, the test must click the sidebar on that page, not reload the original page first.\n    30→\n    31→- For actions that produce side effects (e.g. history entries, timeline updates), write assertions that verify both the primary effect and the side effect. Also assert the side effect happens exactly once — duplicate entries from redundant API calls are a common bug.\n    32→\n    33→- Avoid using `getByText()` with common words that may appear as substrings in other elements (labels,\n    34→  options, buttons). Playwright's `getByText` is case-insensitive and uses substring matching by default.\n    35→  Prefer `getByTestId` for precise element targeting, or use `getByRole`/`getByLabel` with exact matching.\n    36→  A `strict mode violation: getByText(...) resolved to N elements` error means the selector is ambiguous —\n    37→  never work around it with `.first()`, instead use a more specific selector like `getByTestId` or\n    38→  `getByRole` with `{ exact: true }`.\n    39→\n    40→- All `data-testid` values used across all test files for the same component must be consistent. Before\n    41→  writing cross-cutting tests that reference components tested in other spec files, read the existing\n    42→  spec files to use the same testid values.\n    43→\n    44→- When the test spec entry describes file upload functionality (e.g., \"file picker opens\", \"select a\n    45→  file to upload\"), the test must exercise actual file upload mechanics (e.g., Playwright's\n    46→  `setInputFiles` on a file input). Do not substitute a URL/text input test for a file upload test —\n    47→  this masks missing upload functionality in the app.\n    48→\n    49→- NEVER put Playwright locator calls with auto-wait semantics (e.g. `count()`, `textContent()`,\n    50→  `isVisible()`) inside a `.toPass()` retry block in a way that iterates over dynamic elements.\n    51→  If the DOM changes mid-iteration, an inner locator call will auto-wait for an element that no\n    52→  longer exists, and `.toPass()` cannot interrupt that inner wait to retry the block — the test\n    53→  deadlocks. Instead, use a single atomic Playwright assertion that has built-in retry:\n    54→\n    55→  BAD — nested waits deadlock when DOM changes mid-loop:\n    56→  ```ts\n    57→  await expect(async () => {\n    58→    const cards = page.locator('[data-testid^=\"card-\"]');\n    59→    const count = await cards.count();\n    60→    for (let i = 0; i < count; i++) {\n    61→      const text = await cards.nth(i).textContent(); // hangs if DOM shrinks\n    62→      if (text?.includes(name)) found = true;\n    63→    }\n    64→    expect(found).toBeFalsy();\n    65→  }).toPass({ timeout: 15000 });\n    66→  ```\n    67→\n    68→  GOOD — single atomic assertion with built-in retry, no nested waits:\n    69→  ```ts\n    70→  await expect(\n    71→    page.locator('[data-testid^=\"card-\"]').filter({ hasText: name })\n    72→  ).toHaveCount(0, { timeout: 15000 });\n    73→  ```\n    74→\n    75→  The same principle applies to any `.toPass()` block: keep the body free of Playwright auto-waiting\n    76→  calls that can block indefinitely. Use locator chaining (`.filter()`, `.locator()`) and\n    77→  single-assertion expect matchers (`.toHaveCount()`, `.toContainText()`, `.toBeVisible()`) instead.\n    78→\n    79→- Do not add unnecessary state cleanup (e.g., `localStorage.removeItem`, `page.reload()`) in\n    80→  `beforeEach` hooks when Playwright already provides a fresh browser context per test. Redundant\n    81→  cleanup wastes time and can cause tests to exceed their timeout under recording or CI overhead.\n    82→\n    83→- For assertions that depend on backend round-trips (auth flows, database writes, API calls),\n    84→  use generous timeouts (e.g., `{ timeout: 30000 }`) rather than tight ones. Environments with\n    85→  recording overhead (Replay browser) add significant latency beyond typical local development.\n    86→  A tight timeout that barely passes locally will flake under load or recording.\n    87→\n    88→- For tests that chain multiple user flows in a single test (e.g., signup → signout → signin →\n    89→  verify), add `test.slow()` at the top of the test to triple the default timeout. Multi-step\n    90→  end-to-end flows easily exceed the default 60s timeout under recording and CI environments.\n    91→\n    92→- Skill files are at `/repo/skills/tasks/` and its subdirectories (the repo root), NOT inside\n    93→  the app directory. Always use `/repo/skills/tasks/build/writeTests.md`, etc.\n    94→\n    95→- For deployment tests (tests run against a live deployed site rather than a local dev server):\n    96→  - Always use `data-testid` values that match the actual component markup. Read the component\n    97→    source to verify testid values before writing the test — do not guess.\n    98→  - Handle empty-state scenarios gracefully. A freshly deployed app may have no data, so tests\n    99→    must not assume specific records exist. Check for empty-state UI (e.g., \"No items\" messages)\n   100→    or create test data before asserting on table/list contents.\n   101→  - Use generous timeouts (e.g., `{ timeout: 10000 }`) for element assertions. First-load\n   102→    performance on a cold deploy is slower than local development.\n   103→  - Verify that API endpoints are healthy before running deployment tests. See\n   104→    `skills/scripts/deploy-verification.md`.\n   105→\n   106→- When a test spec entry describes editing or interacting with a specific field (e.g., \"edit client\n   107→  field\", \"change value\"), the test must exercise that exact field with corresponding actions and\n   108→  assertions. Do not write a test that only verifies a subset of the fields or operations mentioned\n   109→  in the spec entry — each described interaction must be tested. A common bug is implementing editing\n   110→  for some fields but not all; only per-field testing catches this.\n   111→\n   112→- Apps with login/signup functionality must have a complete e2e test that exercises the full\n   113→  sign-up and sign-in flow against the real auth backend. The test must create a new account,\n   114→  verify the post-signup state (session established or confirmation required), then sign in\n   115→  with the new credentials and verify the authenticated state. This catches issues like email\n   116→  confirmation requirements, incorrect error handling, and session establishment failures that\n   117→  unit-level or mocked tests miss.\n   118→\n   119→- Auth flows that involve email-based verification (email confirmation, password reset) must\n   120→  have dedicated tests that exercise the real production code path — not the IS_TEST bypass.\n   121→  These tests must run the backend without IS_TEST=true (or with IS_TEST=false) so that actual\n   122→  tokens are generated and stored. The test should call the signup/forgot-password endpoint,\n   123→  query the database directly for the generated token, then hit the confirmation/reset endpoint\n   124→  with that token and verify success. This ensures the full flow works end-to-end: token\n   125→  generation, storage, URL construction, and redemption. Other (non-auth-flow) tests may\n   126→  continue to use IS_TEST=true to bypass auth for convenience.\n   127→\n   128→## Test Design and Database Isolation\n   129→\n   130→Tests run serially (`--workers 1`) against a single `netlify dev` server backed by an ephemeral\n   131→Neon branch. The test script resets the database (truncate + re-seed) between each test.\n   132→\n   133→### Test infrastructure\n   134→\n   135→Do **not** use Playwright's `webServer` config to start the dev server. The test script manages\n   136→the `netlify dev` server lifecycle automatically.\n   137→\n   138→### Database access in functions\n   139→\n   140→All Netlify functions must use the shared `netlify/functions/db.ts` module (see writeApp.md\n   141→directives) rather than importing `@neondatabase/serverless` directly.\n   142→\n   143→### Test design rules\n   144→\n   145→- The database is reset between each test, so each test starts with a clean seed.\n   146→- Never hardcode database IDs in tests. Query the UI or API to discover IDs for the records\n   147→  you need to interact with.\n   148→- Each spec file must contain **20 or fewer** `test()` calls. When a page has more tests,\n   149→  split them across multiple spec files grouped by feature area (e.g., `status-page-containers.spec.ts`,\n   150→  `status-page-webhook.spec.ts`). Consolidate similar tests where possible before splitting.\n   151→- The Playwright config must use `fullyParallel: true`. Do not set `workers: 1`.\n   152→- The Playwright config must **not** include a `webServer` section — the worker fixture handles\n   153→  server lifecycle.\n   154→- The Playwright config must use `replayDevices['Replay Chromium']` from `@replayio/playwright`\n   155→  as the browser project, not standard `devices['Desktop Chrome']`. Tests must run under the\n   156→  Replay browser so that recordings are captured for debugging.\n   157→- Set the Playwright config's global `timeout` to at least 60000ms.\n   158→  The Replay browser has significant recording overhead, and with parallel workers all hitting\n   159→  their dev servers simultaneously, pages can take 25+ seconds to load. A 30s timeout that works\n   160→  locally with standard Chrome will cause widespread flakes under recording.\n   161→\n   162→## Tips\n   163→\n   164→- Before writing tests that interact with shared UI components (ConfirmDialog, modals, dropdowns),\n   165→  read the shared component first to get the exact `data-testid` values. Do not guess test IDs.\n   166→- When adding `data-testid` attributes to a set of components for a page, plan ALL testid additions\n   167→  upfront by reading all components first, then make edits in batches. This avoids repeated re-reads\n   168→  and sequential small edits to the same file.\n   169→- When writing tests for a detail page, read: (1) the Netlify function for the entity, (2) the page\n   170→  component, (3) 2-3 representative section components. Do not exhaustively read every file in the app.\n   171→- If Playwright browser installation fails with permission errors, set `PLAYWRIGHT_BROWSERS_PATH` to\n   172→  a writable directory (e.g., `/home/node/.cache/ms-playwright`) before running `npx playwright install`.\n   173→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Dq1TZiwLFixxvGpyJG1LQ2","session_id":"0c501394-3aea-49f7-985d-e5c6f42e3e50","uuid":"2da7defc-7f62-463e-900c-aeb19c197dff"}
[2026-03-04T20:30:22.448Z] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Dq1TZiwLFixxvGpyJG1LQ2","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! Now I have all the information needed. Let me create a comprehensive summary of the todo-app structure for writing Playwright tests, specifically for the Modal tests.\n\n## Summary: Todo App Structure for Playwright Modal Testing\n\nBased on my comprehensive exploration of the codebase, here's a detailed guide for understanding and writing Playwright tests for the Edit Todo Modal:\n\n### 1. **App Information**\n- **App Name**: `todo-app` (from `/repo/tasks/tasks-app-building-gu9tow.json`)\n- **Location**: `/repo/apps/todo-app/`\n- **Framework**: React 19 with Redux Toolkit for state management\n- **Styling**: Tailwind CSS with Vite build system\n- **Testing Framework**: Playwright v1.58.2\n\n### 2. **Modal Component Location & Structure**\n\n**Main File**: `/repo/apps/todo-app/src/components/EditTodoModal.tsx`\n\nKey features:\n- **Props**: Accepts a `Todo` object and an `onClose` callback\n- **Modal Type**: Dialog overlay with semi-transparent backdrop\n- **Form Fields**:\n  - Title (required text input, `data-testid=\"edit-title\"`)\n  - Priority dropdown (`data-testid=\"edit-priority\"`)\n  - Due Date picker (`data-testid=\"edit-due-date\"`)\n  - Notes textarea (`data-testid=\"edit-notes\"`)\n\n**Important Test IDs**:\n- `edit-modal-backdrop` - The backdrop/overlay for clicking outside\n- `edit-modal` - The modal dialog container\n- `edit-title` - Title input field\n- `title-error` - Error message for title validation\n- `edit-priority` - Priority dropdown button\n- `priority-dropdown-menu` - Priority dropdown menu\n- `priority-option-low`, `priority-option-medium`, `priority-option-high` - Individual priority options\n- `edit-due-date` - Date picker input\n- `edit-notes` - Notes textarea\n- `edit-save` - Save button\n- `edit-cancel` - Cancel button\n\n### 3. **Modal Behavior**\n- Opens when clicking on a todo's text from the main list (in `/repo/apps/todo-app/src/pages/MainTodoList.tsx`)\n- Fields pre-fill with existing todo data\n- Title is required - validation error shown if empty when saving\n- Clicking backdrop (outside modal) closes without saving\n- Cancel button closes without saving\n- Save button persists changes via Redux dispatch to `updateTodo`\n- Modal renders as fixed positioned overlay with centered content\n\n### 4. **Test Data Models**\n\n**Todo Type** (`/repo/apps/todo-app/src/types.ts`):\n```typescript\ninterface Todo {\n  id: string\n  title: string\n  completed: boolean\n  priority: 'low' | 'medium' | 'high'\n  due_date: string | null\n  notes: string | null\n  created_at: string\n  updated_at: string\n}\n```\n\n### 5. **Related Components to Understand**\n\n**Main Page** (`/repo/apps/todo-app/src/pages/MainTodoList.tsx`):\n- Manages the `editingTodo` state\n- Conditionally renders the `EditTodoModal`\n- Passes `onClose` callback to close modal\n\n**Todo List Item** (`/repo/apps/todo-app/src/components/TodoItem.tsx`):\n- Has `data-testid=\"todo-text\"` on the clickable title\n- Calls `onEdit(todo)` when clicked, which opens the modal\n- Displays priority badges and due date information\n\n### 6. **Test Documentation Location**\n\n**Complete Test Specifications**: `/repo/apps/todo-app/docs/tests.md` (Lines 293-398)\n\n**Modal-specific test entries** match these subtask titles:\n- Modal opens with all fields pre-filled from existing todo\n- Modal opens with default values for a todo with minimal data\n- Title field is a required text input\n- Edit the title field and save\n- Priority dropdown shows Low, Medium, and High options\n- Change priority and save\n- Set a due date via the date picker\n- Clear an existing due date\n- Add notes to a todo\n- Edit existing notes\n- Notes textarea supports multiline text\n- Save button saves all changes and closes the modal\n- Save button appearance\n- Cancel button discards changes and closes the modal\n- Cancel button appearance\n- Clicking outside the modal discards changes and closes it\n- Modal displays as a dialog overlay\n\n### 7. **Test Execution Setup**\n\n**Test Script**: `/repo/apps/todo-app/scripts/test.ts`\n- Manages ephemeral Neon database branches for test isolation\n- Starts `netlify dev` on port 8888\n- Seeds database before each test\n- Runs Playwright with `--workers 1` (serial execution)\n- Captures recordings via Replay browser on failures\n\n**Running Tests**:\n```bash\nnpm run test path/to/test.spec.ts\n```\n\n### 8. **Playwright Configuration**\n\n**Current Setup**:\n- Playwright v1.58.2 installed\n- Uses Replay Chromium for recording support (`@replayio/playwright`)\n- Global timeout should be at least 60 seconds\n- Tests run serially with database reset between each test\n- Environment variables: `DATABASE_URL`, `BASE_URL` (http://localhost:8888)\n\n### 9. **Key Component Details for Modal Tests**\n\n**Priority Dropdown Implementation**:\n- Opens/closes with button click\n- Options: Low, Medium, High (mapped to 'low', 'medium', 'high' internally)\n- Uses custom ChevronIcon component for rotation animation\n- Click outside automatically closes dropdown\n\n**Due Date Field**:\n- HTML5 date input (`type=\"date\"`)\n- Empty value means no due date\n- Format: ISO string (YYYY-MM-DD)\n\n**Notes Field**:\n- Textarea with `rows={4}` and `resize-y` enabled\n- Supports multiline text with newlines\n- Optional field\n\n**Validation**:\n- Only title is required\n- Shows `title-error` message if empty on save attempt\n- Error text: \"Title is required\"\n\n### 10. **Database & API Integration**\n\n- Redux thunk `updateTodo` dispatches updates\n- Makes PUT request to `/.netlify/functions/todos/:id`\n- Includes all fields: title, priority, due_date, notes\n- `updated_at` timestamp is automatically updated on backend\n\n### 11. **File Paths Reference**\n- Modal Component: `/repo/apps/todo-app/src/components/EditTodoModal.tsx`\n- Main Page: `/repo/apps/todo-app/src/pages/MainTodoList.tsx`\n- Test Documentation: `/repo/apps/todo-app/docs/tests.md`\n- Package Config: `/repo/apps/todo-app/package.json`\n- Test Script: `/repo/apps/todo-app/scripts/test.ts`\n- Types: `/repo/apps/todo-app/src/types.ts`\n\nThis comprehensive guide provides all the necessary context for writing Playwright tests that verify the Modal functionality matches the specifications in `docs/tests.md`."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: ab1335acbd75ea976 (for resuming to continue this agent's work if needed)\n<usage>total_tokens: 51660\ntool_uses: 40\nduration_ms: 47428</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0c501394-3aea-49f7-985d-e5c6f42e3e50","uuid":"48920c11-472f-4398-a56c-4f685dca4936","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"I need to understand the structure of the tasks app for writing Playwright tests. Please:\n1. Find and read the tasks/tasks-app-building-gu9tow.json to identify the app name\n2. Find the app directory under /repo/apps/ and list its structure\n3. Read docs/tests.md in the app directory to understand what tests need to be written\n4. Read the Playwright config file\n5. Read any existing test files\n6. Read the main page/component files related to a \"Modal\" for editing todos (since the subtasks are about modal interactions for editing todos)\n7. Read any existing test fixture/setup files\n\nFocus on finding the test spec entries that match these subtask titles:\n- Modal opens with all fields pre-filled from existing todo\n- Modal opens with default values for a todo with minimal data\n- Title field is a required text input\n- Edit the title field and save\n- Priority dropdown shows Low, Medium, and High options\n- Change priority and save\n- Set a due date via the date picker\n- Clear an existing due date\n- Add notes to a todo\n- Edit existing notes\n- Notes textarea supports multiline text\n- Save button saves all changes and closes the modal\n- Save button appearance\n- Cance